      <div class="modal-body info-modal-body" data-tabs-initialized="true">
        <div class="tab-content info-modal-content" id="class-a-tab-content">
          <div class="tab-pane fade show active" id="class-a-overview" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="class-a-overview-tab" tabindex="0">
            <p class="p-modal text-justify">Specialized Level* (Cấp Chuyên Sâu* / CCS) is the highest professional-technical level under Vietnam's medical examination and treatment framework, established by the Law on Medical Examination and Treatment (Law No. 15/2023/QH15) and detailed in Decree No. 96/2023/NĐ-CP. It applies to hospitals and health centers with inpatient beds that achieve a self-assessed score of 70 points or higher out of a possible 100 points under the scoring scale in Appendix V of Decree 96/2023/NĐ-CP. In this Specialized Level there is a further subset, the Specialized Level High-Technology (<em>cấp chuyên sâu mức kỹ thuật cao</em>/CCS-TC). Currently, CCS-TC is only designated for four hospitals operating directly under the Ministry of Health: Bạch Mai Hospital, Chợ Rẫy Hospital, Huế Central Hospital, and Ho Chi Minh City University Medical Center.</p>
            <p class="p-modal text-justify">This classification applies equally to public and private hospitals. Assignment to CCS is determined solely by a facility's scored professional and technical capability, not by its administrative tier (central, provincial, or district) or its ownership. Following Vietnam's 2025 administrative reform — which abolished the district level of government and consolidated the country's provinces from 63 to 34 — references to "central," "provincial," or "district" hospital status no longer form the basis for this classification, even though many facilities retain those names.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="tab-pane fade" id="class-a-role" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="class-a-role-tab" tabindex="0">
            <ul>
              <li>Serve as tertiary referral facilities for complex, severe, or highly specialized cases.</li>
              <li>Provide subspecialist medical, surgical, obstetric, pediatric, and emergency services beyond the capacity of Basic Level (<em>cấp cơ bản / </em>CCB) facilities.</li>
              <li>Function as postgraduate and specialist training sites, including residency, master's, and doctoral-level clinical training.</li>
              <li>Transfer specialized and complex techniques to lower-tier hospitals and health facilities.</li>
              <li>Undertake national or ministerial-level medical research, clinical trials, and scientific publication.</li>
              <li>Receive referrals from CCB and Primary Level (<em>cấp ban đầu</em> / <em>CBD</em>) facilities across their catchment region.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="tab-pane fade" id="class-a-services" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="class-a-services-tab" tabindex="0">
            <p class="p-modal text-justify">Assignment to CCS is based on a facility's total score on the Appendix V scoring instrument, which allocates 100 points across four weighted groups: (I) service capacity and scope of professional practice — up to 65 points; (II) participation in medical training — up to 15 points; (III) technical support provided to other facilities — up to 10 points; and (IV) scientific research capacity — up to 10 points. A facility scoring 70 points or above is classified as CCS. Facilities are self-assessed annually and the result is reviewed and formally approved by the Ministry of Health (for hospitals directly under the Ministry) or by the provincial Department of Health (for other public and private hospitals), typically through a dedicated classification council (hội đồng xếp cấp chuyên môn kỹ thuật).</p>
            <ul>
              <li>Represented examples include: Việt Đức Friendship Hospital (80 points), Hanoi Medical University Hospital (83 points), Thai Nguyen National General Hospital (77 points), National Children's Hospital (74 points), and Hanoi's Obstetrics Hospital, Tim Hà Nội Hospital, Vinmec International General Hospital, and Tâm Anh General Hospital among private facilities.</li>
            </ul>
            <p><strong>Typical Bed Capacity</strong></p>
            <ul>
              <li>Large specialized-level hospitals commonly operate 500 to 2,000+ beds (for example, Bạch Mai Hospital).</li>
              <li>Mid-sized cấp chuyên sâu hospitals, particularly newer private facilities, typically range from approximately 200 to 500 beds.</li>
              <li>Bed count is not itself a scoring criterion; it is a general profile indicator associated with facilities at this level.</li>
            </ul>
            <p><strong>Core Capability Areas</strong></p>
            <ul>
              <li>Approval to perform a high proportion of special-category and Type I (loại đặc biệt, loại I) techniques on the Ministry of Health's approved technique list.</li>
              <li>Broad general technique coverage across the Ministry of Health's full technique catalogue.</li>
              <li>Senior clinical staffing with a substantial share of department heads and deputy heads holding doctoral or Level II specialist qualifications.</li>
              <li>Capacity to lead the development of clinical quality standards and to conduct clinical quality audits.</li>
              <li>Authorization to pilot or apply new techniques and methods under Article 92 of the 2023 Law on Medical Examination and Treatment.</li>
            </ul>
            <p><strong>Diagnostic &amp; Support Infrastructure</strong></p>
            <ul>
              <li>Full subspecialty diagnostic and treatment services, including advanced imaging (CT, MRI), interventional and critical care capability.</li>
              <li>Intensive Care Unit and Neonatal Intensive Care Unit.</li>
              <li>Blood bank and transfusion services.</li>
              <li>Postgraduate teaching hospital infrastructure and accredited residency programs.</li>
              <li>Capacity to transfer specialized techniques to affiliated lower-tier facilities.</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="p-modal text-justify"><em><strong>Note:</strong> This classification is aligned with Vietnam's medical examination and treatment licensing and classification framework pursuant to Law No. 15/2023/QH15, Decree No. 96/2023/NĐ-CP (Article 89, Article 90, and Appendix V), and Công văn 5845/BYT-KCB (2024).</em></p>
